"content_id": "19287",
"reference_id": 18455,
"version": "1.0.4",
"title": "Jonah 1:7–8",
"media_type": "Text",
"index_reference": "32001007-32001008",
"language": "eng",
"review_level": "Professional",
"content": "<ul><li><p>God permitted the use of lots for certain purposes, such as making decisions when people sought his will (see <a href=\"https://ref.ly/Lev16:8\">Leviticus 16:8</a>; <a href=\"https://ref.ly/Josh18:6\">Joshua 18:6</a>; <a href=\"https://ref.ly/1Chr26:12-1Chr26:16\">1 Chronicles 26:12–16</a>; <a href=\"https://ref.ly/Acts1:26\">Acts 1:26</a>). However, divination in general, as it was practiced throughout the ancient world, displeased the Lord because it sought guidance apart from him (<a href=\"https://ref.ly/Deut18:9-Deut18:13\">Deuteronomy 18:9–13</a>).</p></li><li><p><span><em>who is responsible for this calamity that is upon us</em>: </span>In the ancient Near East, people often believed that disasters or bad events happened because a god was angry. They assumed that someone had caused offense and needed to be identified.</p></li></ul>",
"associations": {
"passage": [
{
"start_ref": "32001007",
"start_ref_usfm": "JON 1:7",
"end_ref": "32001008",
"end_ref_usfm": "JON 1:8"
}
],
